---
id: D39E39A1-0E66-4AFF-AA1F-19EFDECE96E4
title: Harrison Ch273 Aortic Regurgitation
---

# Ch273 Aortic Regurgitation

- **Definition** - insufficiency of the aortic valve resulting in regurgitation of blood from the Ao to the LV, caused by primary valve disease, or aortic root disease
- **Etiology of AR** - caused by 1) primary valve disease, 2) aortic root disease, or 3) combination of both:
  - **Primary valve disease:**
    - **Congenital malformation of the aortic valve:**
      - <u>Congenital biscuspid aortic valve</u> (BAV) - may develop predominant AR instead of AS
      - <u>Congenital fenestrations of the aortic valve</u> - typically produces only a mild AR
    - **Acquired aortic valve diseases:**
      - <u>Infective endocarditis</u> - perforation or destruction of \>=1 aortic valve leaflet due to IE over either 1) normal valve, or 2) diseased valve
      - <u>Rheumatic heart disease</u> - thickening, deformity and shortening of the aortic valve cusps, often w/ mixed AS and AR, and often in the **presence of rheumatic mitral valve disease**
      - <u>Membranous sub-aortic stenosis</u> - degenerative changes in response to high transvalvular gradient resulting in thickening and scarring of the aortic valve leaflet and secondary AR
      - <u>Prolapse of the aortic valve</u> - occurs in ~15% of VSD, or in isolation due to myxomatous degeneration in a/w mitral and/or tricuspid valve disease
      - <u>Trauma</u> - avulsion or rupture of the aortic cusp represents the most serious lesion in non-penetrating cardiac injury
  - **Primary aortic root disease** - due entirely to marked aortic annular dilatation w/o primary involvement of the valve leaflets, resulting in lack of <u>diastolic coaptation of aortic valve leaflet</u>:
    - <u>Medial degeneration of the aortic valve</u> - may be idiopathic or a/w underlying systemic disease:
      - Idiopathic dilatation of the aorta
      - Severe, chronic HTN
      - Marfan's syndrome
      - Annuloaortic ectasia
      - Osteogenesis imperfecta
    - <u>Aortic dissection</u> - retrograde dissection of the aorta involving the aortic annulus
    - <u>Cardiac syphillis</u> - aortic root dilatation w/ scarring of the aortic valve resulting in acute AR; intimal involvement may narrow the coronary ostia resulting in myocardial ischaemia
    - <u>Ankylosing spondylitis</u> - aortic root dilatation w/ scarring of the aortic valve
    - <u>Aortic aneurysms</u> - e.g. Takayasu's aortitis and giant cell aortitis

  
  ![](../img/major_causes_of_aortic_regurgitation.png)
- **Causes of acute severe AR:**
  - Infective endocarditis
  - Aortic dissection
  - Trauma
  - Aortitis
- **Pathophysiology and haemodynamic consequences of acute, severe AR:**
  - **Haemodynamic consequences of acute regurgitation of the aortic valve** - increased LVEDP and LVEDV accounts for the major haemodynamic consequence of AR:
    - <u>Reverse diastolic pressure gradient from Ao to LV drives regurgitation</u> - regurgitation occurs in **diastoic phase**, driven by the pressure gradient between AoP and LVEDP (accounting for the decrescendo nature of the diastolic murmur)
    - <u>Effective stroke volume is less than total stroke volume</u> - total LV stroke volume (increased in AR), becomes the sum of effective forward stroke volume, and the volume of blood regurgitate back into the LV (effective forward stroke volume equates reguritant volume in some severe AR)
    - <u>Equilibration of Ao pressure and LVEDP</u> - insufficient time for dilatation of LV, resulting in **increased LVEDP** (\> 40 mmHg)
  - **Rapid runoff of AoP** - regurgitant jets into the LV results in rapid decrease in diastolic pressure (wide pulse pressure), which comprimises coronary blood flow
  - **Effects of raised LVEDP** - results in premature closure MV (?functionally MS), rapid raise of LA pressure, pulmonary artery wedge pressures and <u>APO</u>
- **Pathophysiology and haemodynamic consequences of chronic, severe AR:**
  - **Dilatation and eccentric hypertrophy in response to raised LVEDV** - allows chamber to <u>increase total stroke volume</u> w/o relative shortening of myofibrils:
    - <u>Adequate compensation</u> - results in LVEF and effective stroke volume, which **prevents further raising of LVEDV and LVEDP**
    - <u>Increased wall tension and afterload</u> - by Laplace law, significant increase in radius from LV dilatation (but offset by hyertrophy) results in increased LV systolic tension required to develop same level of systemic pressure
    - <u>LV decompensation</u> - results in further rise in LVEDP, and a decline in LVEF
  - **Effects of LV decompensation** - patients generally asymptomatic until LV decompensation occurs:
    - <u>Reduced LVEF</u> (first sign of decompensation) - reduced total stroke volume, and hence reduced effective forward stroke volume resulting in reduced CO at rest
    - <u>Further elevation of LVEDP</u> - premature closure of MV, w/ elevation of LA, pulmonary artery wedge, pulmonary artery and RV pressure
- **Clinical features of acute severe AR** - insufficient time for compensation resulting in rapid elevation of LVEDP:
  - <u>Acute pulmonary oedema</u> - rapid development of APO due to raised LVEDP, raised LA pressures
  - <u>Cardiogenic shock</u> - LV dysfunction w/ reduction of LVEF, together w/ regurgitation volume, results in **markedly reduced effective forward stroke volume**
- **Clinical features of chronic severe AR** - patients may remain asymptomatic for as long as 10-15y:
  - **Palpitations** (usually first complaint) - usually an unplessant awareness of the bounding heart beat, which may be a/w head bobbing, especially when lying down or w/ sinus tachycardia/ PVC:
    - <u>Palpitations when lying down</u> - possibly related to further **increase in preload from increased venous return**
    - <u>Papitations with sinus tachycardia</u> - by means of increasing frequency
    - <u>Palpitations with PVC</u> - increased diastolic filling time (from LA and reverse pressure gradient from Ao)
  - **Dyspnoea and features of HF:**
    - <u>SOBOE</u> - usually the first feature of deminished cardiac reserve
    - <u>Orthopnea and PND</u> - seen in advanced stage chronic AR
    - <u>Oedema</u> - systemic fluid accumulation, including congestive hepatomegaly and ankle edema may develop late in the disease course
  - **Chest pains** - anginal chest pains even in the absence of coronary artery disease due to 1) increased myocardial O2 demand from hypertrophy and increased systolic tension, and 2) comprimised coronary supply due to rapid runoff of AoP:
    - <u>Timing</u> - less predictable compared w/ CAD:
      - Exertional
      - At rest
      - Nocturnal angina (?increased preload resulting in increased myocardial demand)
    - <u>Provacating and relieving factors</u> - often does **not respond satisfactory to sublingual TNG**
- **Signs:**
  - **General examination** - reveals conditions predisposing to AR:
    - <u>Infective endocarditis</u> - hand signs of infective endocarditis
    - <u>Marfan's syndrome</u> - high-arched palate
    - <u>Ankylosing spondylitis</u> - spine deformity
  - **Peripheral signs** - rare in mild AR but theoretically possible in chronic severe AR, related to severe palpitations:
    - <u>De musset sign</u> - jarring of the entire body and the bobbing motion of the head w/ each systole
    - <u>Quinke's pulse</u> - alternating flushing and paling of the skin at the nail root while pressure is applied to the tip of the nail
    - <u>Traube's sign</u> - booming pistol-shot sound heard over femoral arteries
    - <u>Duroziez's sign</u> - audible to-and-fro murmur in the femoral arteries
  - **Arterial pulse:**
    - **Heart rate** - invariably tachycardic to preserve CO
    - **Character of arterial pulse:**
      - <u>Collapsing pulse</u> - re-emergence of bounding pulse upon raising the hand related to increased pulse pressure from increased venous return
      - <u>Corrigan's pulse</u> - visible rising and sudden collapse w/ systole and diastole best seen in the carotid region
  - **Palpation of the precordium:**
    - <u>Displacement of the apex beat</u> - displaced laterally and inferiorly corresponding to the dilatation
    - <u>Thrusting apex beat</u> - prominent systolic expansion and diastolic retraction
    - <u>Thrills</u> - diastolic thrill at the LLSB (w/ systolic thrill at carotids)
  - **Auscultation:**
    - <u>S1 and S2</u> - A2 deminished in severe chronic AR (S1 soft in acute AR due to early closure of MV)
    - <u>S3 and S4</u> - occasionally an S4 may be heard due to premature closure of the mitral valve
    - <u>Early decrescendo diastolic murmur</u>:
      - Best heard at the LLSB (if louder on the right, may be due to aneuurysmal dilatation of the aortic root)
      - Increased w/ maneuvers a/w increased venous return (e.g. leaning forward in full inspiration)
      - Increased w/ maneuvers that increase afterload (e.g. strenous and sustained handgrip)
    - <u>Mid systolic ejection murmur</u> - likely flow murmur:
      - Best heard at the base of the heart (i.e. aortic area)
      - Radiates towards bilateral carotids
      - However of different character compared w/ the ejection systolic murmur in AS
    - <u>Austin Flint murmur</u> - likely due to pre-mature closing of the MV in response to elevated LVEDP:
      - Mid-to-late diastolic murmur best heard at the apex
- **Ix:**
  - **ECG:**
    - <u>Signs of LV hypertrophy</u> - Sokolow criteria, S wave in V1 + R wave in V6 \> 35 mm, and LAD
    - <u>LV strain pattern</u> - ST-depression and TWI in I, aVL, V5-6
  - **Echocardiography:**
    - <u>Assessment of LVEF</u>:
      - Compensated chronic AR will have normal or even supranormal LVEF
      - LV decompensation is signalled by a decrease in EF or an increase in the end-systolic dimensions
    - <u>Assessment of valvular morphology</u>:
      - Rapid high frequency diastolic fluttering of the anterior mitral leaflet produced by the impact of the regurgitant jet
      - Aortic valve insufficiency observed
    - <u>Doppler findings</u>:
      - Central regurgitant tract \> 65% of LVOT
      - Regurgitant volume \> 60mL/ beat (or regurgitant fraction \>= 50%)
      - Diastolic flow reversal in the proximal descending thoracic aorta

      
      ![](../img/echo_and_doppler_of_severe_ar.png)
  - **Additional Ix:**
    - <u>CXR</u> - cardiomegaly (LV displaced posteriorly and may encrouch the spine on lateral view)
    - <u>Cardiac catheterisation</u> - for confirmation of magnitude of regurgitant jet, w/ evaluation of the coronaries prior to surgery
- **Mx:**
